!v060!595.2(826.9)
!v062!CIENCIAS BIOLOGICAS
!v062!ZOOLOGIA
!v062!INVERTEBRADOS
!v062!ENTOMOLOGIA
!v062!INSECTOS
!v062!THYSANOPTERA
!v062!THRIPS
!v062!ARTROPODOS
!v062!JUJUY
!v062!PREPUNA
!v062!TESIS
!v069!Thysanoptera re£ne cerca de 5.500 especies agrupadas en dos sub¢rdenes. Terebrantia y Tubulifera que incluyen nueve familias y 750 gÇneros. Los objetivos de esta tesis son: realizar estudios sistem†ticos y bioecol¢gicos de los tisan¢pteros presentes en los cultivos, vegetaci¢n espontanea y silvestre. Para su estudio en la provincia de Jujuy se seleccionaron las zonas semi†ridas y †ridas (Prepuna y Puna) ubicadas entre 1300 y 6200 m s.n.m. Se realizaron descripciones en c†maras de cr°a bajo condiciones controladas de laboratorio. Se identifican y caracterizan cuatro familias (Aeolothripidae, Heterothripidae, Thripidae y Aeolothripidae), 21 gÇneros y 35 especies, confeccion†ndose claves para el reconocimiento de las mismas. Se describen un gÇnero y cinco especies nuevas y tres morfoespecies de Haplothrips Amyot & Serville; se citan por primera vez para la Argentina los gÇneros Anaphothrips Uzel, Psilothrips Hood, Scopaeothrips Hood y Pseudophilothrips Johansen y se establece una nueva sinonimia: Desertathrips chuquiraga De Borb¢n a Pseudothrips chuquiraga (De Borb¢n). Se describe e ilustra la larva II de seis especies. Se determina por primera vez el ciclo de vida de Frankliniella tuberosi Moulton  Frankliniella jujuyensis sp. nov., Neohydatothrips denigratus (De Santis), Heterothrips cacti Hood y Pseudophilothrips lifolia sp. nov.; a partir de muestreos peri¢dicos en cultivos, vegetaci¢n espont†nea y silvestre se amplia el n£mero de plantas hospedadoras de la mayor°a de las especies de tisan¢pteros analizadas. El 89 por ciento de las especies obtenidas son fit¢fagas (ant¢filas, fol°voras y de dieta mixta), el 8 por ciento depredadoras y 3 por ciento fung°voras,. Las especies que inciden en la sanidad de los cultivos son: Frankliniella australis Morgan, Frankliniella tuberosi, Frankliniella schultzei Trybom, Frankliniella occidentalis (Pergaande) y Thrips tabaaci Lindeman. La vegetaci¢n espont†nea alberga el mismo complejo de tisan¢pteros asociado a cultivos proporcion†ndoles una fuente alternativa de alimento y/o refugio. De acuerdo con los niveles de especificidad tisan¢ptero-planta, se distiguen especies generalistas (pol°fagas) y especialistas (olig¢fagas). La £nica especie gal°cola registrada es Pseudophilothrips lifolia sp.. nov. que podr°a comportarse como biocontroladora de la planta hemopar†sita Ligaria cuneifolia (Ruiz et Pavon). A fin de lograr el manejo de T. tabaci, especie perjudicial al cultivo de ajo, se aplicaron tÇcnicas agroecol¢gicas brindando a los productores una alternativa factible y econ¢mica al uso de agroqu°micos en este cultivo de gran valor comercial para la regi¢n estudiada. ABSTRACT Thysanoptera comprises about 5500 species, grouped into two suborders: Terebrantia and Tubulifera which include nine families and 750 genera. The present study was carried out in the province of Jujuy, Argentina, in the seminiarid and arid areas (Prepuna and Puna) located between 1300 and 6200 m.a.s.l. Its objectives were systematic and bioecological analyses of Thysanoptera affecting crops and spontaneous uncultivated vegetation of this area. Descriptions and illustrations of adult and immature specimens (Larva II) were made. Identification and characterization of four families (Aeolothripidae, Heterothripidae. Thripidae y Phlaeothripidae), 21 genera and 35 speces were achieved, providing keys for their identification. A new genus, five new species and three morphospecies of Haplothrips Amyot & Serville were described; Anaphothrips Uzel, Psilothrips Hood, Scopaeothrips Hood and Pseudophilothrips Johansen genera were studied for the first time in Argentina; and a new Desertathrips chuquiraga De Borb¢n to Pseudothrips chuquiraga (De Borb¢n) combination was established. Larva II stage of six species was described and illustrated. Biological studies were made in breeding chambers under controlled laboratory conditions, which allowed to determine the life cycle of Frankliniella tuberosi Moulton, Frankliniella jujyensis sp. nov., Neohydatothrips denigratus (De Santis), Heterothrips cati Hood and Pseudophilothrips lifolia sp. nov. for the first time. Based on periodical samplings in cropss and spontaneous uncultivated vegetation, new associations were established, increasing the number of host plants for the majority of the Thysanoptera under consideration. Our findings determined that 89 per cent of the species collected were phytophagic or plant-eating (anthophyle, folivore and of mixed diet), 8 per cent predators and 3 per cent fungus-eating ones. Species found whit incidence in crop health were Frankliniella australis, Frankliniella schultzei, Frankliniella occidentalis, F. tuberosi and Thrips tabaci. Spontaneous vegetation hosts the same complex of Thysanoptera associated to crops by providing them alternative food/refuge sources. Generalist (polyphagous) and specilist (oligophaous and monophagous) species were distinguished according to the level of specificity between Thysanoptera and plant. Pseudophilothrips lifolia sp. nov. was the only gallicolous species recorded that  could behave as a bio.comptroller of the hemiparasitic Ligaria cuneifolia (Ruiz et Pavon). In order to manage T. tabaci, the species that affects garlic crops, agro-ecological techniques were used, providing producers a feasible and economic alternative to conventional agrochemical products.
